    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe rename/unification itself is coherent and faithful: `AssignMemberId` cleanly absorbs the two former steps, the YAML wiring drops the extra step, the mapper's now-redundant `toCheckDigit` is removed, and every doc comment (`CityRegions`, `FiscalYear`, `LuhnCheckDigit`, `OwnerTelephones`, `OwnerCreatedEvent`, OpenAPI, schemas) is updated consistently. Combining region+FY+hash+check into one assignment unit is the right call given the spec, and `OwnerCreatedEvent`/`AuditOwnerCreated` correctly re-point to the single `memberId`.\n\nOne structural finding:\n\n**`AssignMemberId` re-implements SHA-256 instead of reusing the codebase's shared `Hashing` primitive.**\n- `src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/AssignMemberId.java` copies a private `hash8()` that does `MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\")`, the UTF-8 byte loop, and `NoSuchAlgorithmException` handling.\n- The model package already contains `Hashing.sha256Hex(String)`, whose own javadoc states it is *\"the SHA-256 primitive shared by the owner-identity derivation helpers \u2026 so the hashing lives in one place rather than being copied.\"* `OwnerIdentity` \u2014 the very \"region-and-hash identity\" this class's javadoc says HASH8 must match \u2014 derives its hash through `Hashing`.\n- Every other segment of the new class already routes through the codebase's single-definition helpers (`CityRegions` for REGION, `FiscalYear` for FY, `LuhnCheckDigit` for CHK, `OwnerTelephones` for the phone). Rolling a private SHA-256 for the hash segment alone is the one place that breaks that convention, and it directly contradicts the \"don't copy the hashing\" intent a maintainer of this codebase relies on.\n- Suggested shape: `hash8` becomes `Hashing.sha256Hex(normalizedTelephone + lastName).substring(0, 8).toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT)`, dropping the copied `MessageDigest`/`NoSuchAlgorithmException`/import boilerplate entirely.\n\n(This duplication was carried over verbatim from the deleted `AssignCustomerCode`, but since a brand-new class is being written here and the surrounding refactor otherwise diligently reuses shared helpers, this is the natural moment to route it through `Hashing`.)",
